`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"Two paths to the same detector can give a quantum particle a lower probability of arriving than either path alone. The contributions from the paths have directions as well as sizes: they add like arrows, and opposite arrows cancel. Electromagnetism can change that cancellation by changing the relative phase accumulated along the paths. Fiber bundles describe how those phases are compared from place to place."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"Below, the blue arrow stays fixed while the amber arrow turns. Their sum is violet. Turn the relative phase halfway around and watch the sum shrink to zero. The bar measures its squared length, normalized so that aligned arrows give one."}),`
