`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"The transverse choice in the figure is one family of solutions. For a vacuum plane wave, the divergence equations themselves require the varying electric and magnetic fields to be perpendicular to its propagation direction. That physical constraint comes from Maxwell's equations, not from drawing the arrows sideways."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.h2,{children:"The scope of the geometric description"}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"A string displacement, a charged amplitude, and an electromagnetic connection are different physical objects. Bundle language helps organize them: values live in fibers, fields select values, connections compare them, and curvature measures local failure of transport to agree around loops. The equations governing those objects still have to be supplied and checked against experiment."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"That distinction is what makes the framework useful. It separates a change of description from a change in the field, and separates the field's geometric structure from the physical law that evolves it. It also explains why a description of light needs no material carrying displacement from one point to the next. The field has its own dynamics; no hidden string is part of Maxwell's vacuum equations."}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The phrase “universal medium” was the provocation behind the first version of this article, inspired by ",s.jsx(e.a,{href:"https://x.com/ericweinstein/status/2072746828731384136",children:"Eric Weinstein's post"}),". Taken as a shared mathematical language for fields, it points toward a substantial connection. Taken as a physical substance, or as a claim that local phase conventions alone force light to exist, it goes beyond the argument."]}),`
`,s.jsx(e.h2,{children:"Further reading"}),`
`,s.jsxs(e.p,{children:[s.jsx(e.a,{href:"https://journals.aps.org/prd/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevD.12.3845",children:"Wu and Yang's 1975 paper"})," gives the explicit dictionary between gauge fields and connections on principal bundles. Its treatment of phase transport and overlapping descriptions is the technical foundation of the middle of this article."]}),`
