`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"The failure of the 1913 theory was not inevitable. It was a serious proposal that Einstein continued to defend despite a known quantitative shortfall. Its eventual rejection required reconsidering assumptions and calculations, not simply waiting for Mercury to announce a verdict."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"What survived was substantial: gravity represented by a spacetime metric, free fall described geometrically, and tensor calculus used to connect physical measurements across coordinate descriptions. Grossmann helped develop that structure while the correct field equation was still unknown. Besso helped carry a demanding astronomical calculation far enough to test it."}),`
`,s.jsx(e.p,{children:"The distinction between the structure and the law remains useful when reading the finished theory. A metric tells clocks and rulers what they measure. Its connection defines free fall. Its curvature controls tidal effects. The field equation determines which of those geometries can accompany a given distribution of matter. Mercury's orbit tests their combination."}),`
